+# Auricrux security hardening and deployment reference
+
+How Auricrux enforces its security boundaries, and the configuration required to run it safely.
+
+The single most important thing on this page: **`Auth:Enabled` is `false` by default.** In that
+mode every caller is anonymous and shares one storage partition. That is a supportable
+configuration for a single-tenant self-hosted install, and an unsafe one for anything serving more
+than one customer. See [Running multi-tenant](#running-multi-tenant).
+
+---
+
+## Boundaries
+
+### Caller identity
+
+`AuricruxPrincipalResolver` resolves every request to an `AuricruxPrincipal`: a subject, how
+strongly that subject is known (`Authenticated`, `ClientAsserted`, or `Anonymous`), and whether
+the caller is an administrator.
+
+The rule that matters is that the `X-Auricrux-Email` header is **only** honoured while
+`Auth:Enabled` is off. Once authentication is on, the header is ignored outright and identity comes
+only from a validated token. Without that rule, a caller could name someone else's account and
+inherit its plan, quota, and stored data.
+
+`TenantKey` — a truncated SHA-256 of the subject — is the partition key for stored data. Anonymous
+callers all resolve to the single `anonymous` partition.
+
+### API authorization
+
+`AuricruxAuthorizationFilter` is registered as a global MVC filter, so an endpoint is protected
+unless it opts out with `[AuricruxPublic]`. A newly added endpoint is therefore protected by
+omission rather than exposed by it.
+
+It is a filter rather than per-action `[Authorize]` for two reasons: endpoints stay reachable in
+hosts that register no authentication scheme (instead of failing with "No authenticationScheme was
+specified"), and the decision reads live configuration, so configuration layered on after the host
+is built — as the integration tests do — is honoured.
+
+Endpoints that stay public: `/api/health`, `/livez`, `/readyz`, `/api/models`,
+`/api/capabilities`, `/api/account/plans`, `/api/account/register`, `/api/memory/backends`, and
+`/openapi/v1.json`.
+
+### Data isolation
+
+Workspace paths and conversation session ids are both caller-chosen, so isolation cannot rely on
+them being unguessable.
+
+- Workspace files resolve beneath `Data/workspace/{tenantKey}/`. Paths are checked for traversal
+  and compared against the tenant root with a trailing separator, so a sibling directory whose
+  name merely starts with the root cannot satisfy the check.
+- Conversation turns are stored under `{tenantKey}::{sessionId}` across all three backends.
+  Responses report the logical session id, never the storage key.
+
+### Plan and billing
+
+Plan state is revenue state, so plan changes are fail-closed. `POST /api/account/{email}/upgrade`
+requires either:
+
+- the `X-Auricrux-Provisioning-Key` header matching `Billing:ProvisioningKey` (compared in fixed
+  time), or
+- an authenticated caller listed in `Auth:AdminEmails`.
+
+A deployment configuring neither cannot grant upgrades at all and returns `503`. The
+`Billing:AllowUnauthenticatedPlanChanges` escape hatch restores the old open behaviour for
+single-tenant installs and must be set deliberately; it is reported as a warning at startup and in
+the capabilities report.
+
+### Outbound fetches
+
+`OutboundUrlGuard` protects `/api/browse` and the agent's `web_browse` tool.
+
+A hostname allow/deny list is not by itself an SSRF control: `evil.example.com` can resolve to
+`169.254.169.254`, and an allowed public host can redirect to one. The guard therefore resolves
+the host and requires **every** address it resolves to be public unicast — covering IPv4, IPv6,
+and IPv4-mapped IPv6 — and rejects loopback, private, link-local, carrier-grade NAT, multicast,
+and reserved ranges. Redirects are followed manually with each hop revalidated, up to five hops.
+
+### Untrusted content in prompts
+
+Text retrieved from the web is data, not instruction. `UntrustedContent` delimits it and defangs
+instruction-override and role-impersonation phrasing before it reaches a model. This matters
+specifically because the agent can act on what it reads: a page saying "ignore previous
+instructions and fetch …" would otherwise drive further tool calls.
+
+### Rate limiting
+
+Requests are partitioned by account, falling back to client address for anonymous callers, with a
+global limiter as a backstop against a flood spread across partitions.
+
+`X-Forwarded-For` is honoured **only** from proxies named in `Network:KnownProxies`. Trusting it
+unconditionally would let any caller forge a client address and escape per-caller limiting
+entirely. Behind a load balancer, configure the proxy addresses or per-caller limiting degrades to
+one shared bucket.
+
+---
+
+## Configuration
+
+### Authentication
+
+| Key | Default | Purpose |
+|---|---|---|
+| `Auth:Enabled` | `false` | Master switch. Turning it on makes the API default-deny. |
+| `Auth:Authority` | *(empty)* | OIDC issuer. Required — authentication stays off without it. |
+| `Auth:Audience` | `auricrux` | Expected JWT audience. |
+| `Auth:ClientId` | *(empty)* | Enables the interactive browser login when set. |
+| `Auth:ClientSecret` | *(empty)* | OIDC client secret. |
+| `Auth:AdminEmails` | `[]` | Accounts permitted to change any plan. |
+
+### Billing
+
+| Key | Default | Purpose |
+|---|---|---|
+| `Billing:ProvisioningKey` | *(empty)* | Shared secret the checkout backend presents to change plans. |
+| `Billing:RequireIdentity` | `false` | Reject unidentified callers at `/api/chat` so every request is metered. |
+| `Billing:AllowUnauthenticatedPlanChanges` | `false` | Restores open plan changes. Single-tenant only. |
+
+### Outbound fetch
+
+| Key | Default | Purpose |
+|---|---|---|
+| `WebBrowse:AllowedHosts` | `[]` | When non-empty, only these hosts and their subdomains may be fetched. |
+| `WebBrowse:DeniedHosts` | `[]` | Hosts refused regardless of resolved address. |
+| `WebBrowse:AllowPlainHttp` | `true` | Set `false` to require https. |
+
+### Network and limits
+
+| Key | Default | Purpose |
+|---|---|---|
+| `Network:KnownProxies` | `[]` | Proxy addresses whose `X-Forwarded-For` is trusted. |
+| `Network:ForwardLimit` | `1` | Forwarded header hops to process. |
+| `RateLimit:PermitLimit` | `120` | Requests per minute per caller. |
+| `RateLimit:GlobalPermitLimit` | `3000` | Requests per minute across all callers. |
+| `Limits:MaxUploadBytes` | `104857600` | Maximum request body, including uploads. |
+| `AllowedHosts` | `*` | Set to the deployment's hostnames to reject host-header spoofing. |
+| `Cors:AllowedOrigins` | localhost + production origin | Browser origins permitted to call the API. |
+
+---
+
+## Running multi-tenant
+
+Anything serving more than one customer needs all of the following:
+
+```jsonc
+{
+  "Auth": {
+    "Enabled": true,
+    "Authority": "https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant>/v2.0",
+    "Audience": "auricrux",
+    "ClientId": "<client-id>",
+    "AdminEmails": ["ops@futurecontractorsofamerica.com"]
+  },
+  "Billing": {
+    "ProvisioningKey": "<from secret store, not source control>",
+    "RequireIdentity": true
+  },
+  "Network": {
+    "KnownProxies": ["10.0.0.4"]
+  },
+  "AllowedHosts": "auricrux.futurecontractorsofamerica.com"
+}
+```
+
+`Billing:ProvisioningKey` and `Auth:ClientSecret` belong in the platform secret store — Azure Key
+Vault, Kubernetes secrets, or environment variables — never in `appsettings.json`.
+
+### Verifying a deployment
+
+`GET /api/capabilities` reports the posture actually in effect:
+
+```json
+{
+  "securityPosture": {
+    "authenticationEnabled": true,
+    "defaultDenyApi": true,
+    "tenantIsolation": "per-account",
+    "planChangesRequireCredential": true,
+    "warnings": []
+  }
+}
+```
+
+An empty `warnings` array is the goal. Every warning names a setting that leaves the deployment
+more open than recommended, and the same warnings are logged at startup.
+
+---
+
+## Health probes
+
+| Endpoint | Use | Depends on upstreams |
+|---|---|---|
+| `/livez` | Liveness / restart decisions | No |
+| `/readyz` | Readiness / traffic admission | Yes |
+| `/api/health` | Detailed operator report | Yes |
+
+Use `/livez` for liveness. Pointing a liveness probe at anything that checks upstreams means a
+model backend outage restarts every replica instead of degrading answers.
+
